\section*{Linear c0 detection from bi-Lipschitz absorption}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 324; 2026-09-26), the asserted result is that a separable real Banach space with no linear copy of $c_0$ is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to its $c_0$ sum and contains bi-Lipschitz images of every separable metric space.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Let $c_0$ be real null sequences with the supremum norm, and use the maximum norm on a direct sum. The manuscript claims existence of a separable real Banach space $Z$ and a surjective map
\[
 F:Z\oplus_{\infty} c_0\longrightarrow Z
\]
that is bi-Lipschitz, although no closed linear subspace of $Z$ is linearly isomorphic to $c_0$. Thus this absorption is metric rather than a linear direct-sum identity.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 324; 2026-09-26), a state-dependent coordinate shift is made onto by finite-dimensional terminal correction. Molecule norms combine selected Lipschitz tests with exponents tending to one in an outer square sum; compatible detectors rule out a linear $c_0$ basis while retaining nonlinear absorption.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 324; 2026-09-26).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 324; 2026-09-26), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: a separable real Banach space with no linear copy of $c_0$ is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to its $c_0$ sum and contains bi-Lipschitz images of every separable metric space.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{C0Absorption.lean}, \texttt{LipschitzEquivalence.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.C0Absorption.main\_result}, \texttt{OAI.LipschitzCounterexample.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

